from common import ROOT
import re
p=ROOT/'revision/review_v3/manuscript.md';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8');old=s
changes=[
('Australian prediction research already combines dynamical and statistical information. Calibration, bridging and merging methods use climate predictors alongside dynamical forecasts for seasonal rainfall (Schepen et al. 2014).','Australian calibration, bridging and merging methods combine climate predictors with dynamical seasonal rainfall forecasts (Schepen et al. 2014).'),
('ACCESS-S2 supplies an operational subseasonal to seasonal prediction system (Wedd et al. 2022). Recent studies connect its performance to northern rainy-season retreat, southwestern growing-season rainfall and atmospheric moisture transport (Cowan et al. 2024; Firth et al. 2024; Reid et al. 2024).','ACCESS-S2 provides operational subseasonal to seasonal forecasts (Wedd et al. 2022), with studies examining northern rainy-season retreat, southwestern growing-season rainfall and moisture transport (Cowan et al. 2024; Firth et al. 2024; Reid et al. 2024).'),
('The remaining question for this application is what direct aggregate forecasts contribute after the weekly distributions are held fixed. A lower score after reconciliation can reflect useful aggregate predictors, additional hierarchy nodes or changes in forecast dependence. Climate information introduced at the weekly scale may also supply the same benefit through simple aggregation. Distinguishing these explanations requires comparisons that hold the other forecast components constant.','The question is what direct aggregates contribute when weekly distributions are fixed. Lower reconciled scores can reflect aggregate predictors, hierarchy nodes or forecast dependence. Climate-informed weeks may supply the same benefit through summation. Controlled comparisons distinguish these explanations.'),
('A small diagonal ridge ensures numerical invertibility. The shrinkage calculation and matrices are archived for every fold.',"A small diagonal ridge ensures invertibility; each fold's shrinkage calculation and matrix are archived."),
('Because deterministic quantile samples are not independent random draws, matched-size ECC comparisons provide the direct control for dependence; fair CRPS alone is not treated as a complete remedy for all sampling differences.','Deterministic quantiles are not independent random draws, so matched-size ECC comparisons control dependence directly; fair CRPS alone cannot resolve all sampling differences.'),
('These comparisons use identical marginal quantiles within each dependence scheme. Absolute finite-sample scores and interval widths differ, so they support the local direction of the climate contrast rather than equivalence of the dependence models.','Marginal quantiles are identical within each scheme. Different absolute scores and widths support the local climate contrast without establishing equivalence between dependence models.'),
('The fixed regularisation settings keep the forecast family unchanged and specify predictor flexibility before validation. H1 therefore tests a defined climate adjustment within the existing forecast model.','Fixed regularisation specifies predictor flexibility before validation and keeps the forecast family unchanged.')]
for a,b in changes:
 assert s.count(a)==1,a
 s=s.replace(a,b)
assert re.findall(r'\d+(?:\.\d+)?',old)==re.findall(r'\d+(?:\.\d+)?',s)
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8');print('Removed',len(old.split())-len(s.split()),'further words without changing numerical tokens.')
